      <h2 style="font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:28px 0 12px">How to use Unlock PDF</h2>
      <ol style="padding-left:20px;margin:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px">
        <li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Open the unlocker:</strong> Go to ihatepdf.cv/unlock-pdf. No account, no email, nothing to install.</li><li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Upload the PDF:</strong> The document is inspected on your own device and you are shown which actions it currently blocks.</li><li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Review what it blocks:</strong> Printing, copying, editing, annotating, form-filling, screen-reader access, page rearranging and high-quality printing are each listed as allowed or blocked.</li><li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Remove and download:</strong> Click to remove the restrictions and download an unrestricted copy. No watermark.</li>
      </ol>
      
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">Why PDF restrictions are weaker than they look</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">PDF permissions are one of the more misunderstood parts of the format. When a document is protected with an owner password, its content is not sealed away — the permission flags are stored in the file and viewers are expected to honour them voluntarily. Adobe Reader will refuse to print a document flagged no-print; a different viewer may simply ignore the flag. That is why the same PDF can print from one application and not another, and why the restriction can be removed without knowing any password. This is genuinely different from a user password, where the page content is encrypted and no amount of re-saving will reveal it. If you need real protection, permissions are not it — use the encrypt tool to set an open password instead.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">When you legitimately need to do this</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">The situations come up constantly in ordinary work. A supplier sends a specification you need to print for a site visit. An old internal report was locked by someone who left the company years ago. A form arrives with form-filling disabled by mistake. A document blocks screen-reader access, which makes it unusable for a colleague who relies on one — a case where the restriction is not just inconvenient but exclusionary. A licensed template you paid for prevents the editing you bought it to do. In each case the restriction is an obstacle rather than a protection, and removing it is entirely reasonable. What is not reasonable is stripping protection from material you have no right to use, and that remains your call rather than the tool's.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">What to do instead if you want real protection</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">If you are on the other side of this problem — trying to stop people copying your document — permissions will not achieve it, because they can be removed in seconds by anyone who wants to. Meaningful options are: encrypt the PDF with an open password so it cannot be read without the password; flatten the document so form fields and annotations become permanent page content; export pages as images so there is no text layer to copy; or watermark it so copies are traceable back to the recipient. None of these make a document uncopyable — nothing does once someone can see it on screen — but each is substantially stronger than a permission flag.</p>
      </div>
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:32px 0 14px">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px">
        
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">How do I remove restrictions from a PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Upload it at ihatepdf.cv/unlock-pdf, review the list of what the document blocks, and click to remove the restrictions. You get back an unrestricted copy that prints, copies and edits normally. It is free, needs no sign-up, and the file is never uploaded to a server.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">What is the difference between an owner password and a user password?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">A user password stops the PDF opening at all — the content is genuinely encrypted and cannot be read without it. An owner password leaves the file fully readable but sets permission flags asking viewers to block printing, copying or editing. Because the content is not encrypted around those flags, they can be cleared by re-saving the document. This tool handles the second case; for the first you need the actual password and the Remove Password tool.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Why can I open the PDF but not print it?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">The document has permission flags set by an owner password. Your viewer is honouring a request from the file to disable printing. The page content itself is not protected — which is why removing the flags restores printing without needing any password.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Is this legal?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">It depends entirely on the document and your rights to it. Removing restrictions from your own documents, or ones you have permission to use, is a normal and legitimate need — restrictions get applied by software you no longer have a licence for, by a colleague who has left, or by a supplier who never intended to block you. Circumventing protection on material you have no rights to may be unlawful where you live. The tool does not judge that; you are responsible for having the right to unlock what you upload.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Will unlocking change how the document looks?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">No. The page content, fonts, images and layout are untouched. Only the permission flags are removed, so the file looks identical and simply stops refusing to print or copy.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Can it remove a password I do not know?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">No. If the PDF requires a password to open, the content is encrypted and cannot be read without that password. The tool detects this case and tells you rather than pretending to work. Any service claiming otherwise is either guessing weak passwords or misleading you.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Does it work on scanned PDFs?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Yes. Restrictions are a property of the document container, not of what is on the pages, so it makes no difference whether the content is real text or a scan.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Is my document uploaded to a server?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">No. Inspection and re-saving both happen inside your browser tab. Restricted documents are often contracts, reports and licensed material, which is exactly the category you should not be uploading to an unknown service to unlock.</p>
        </div>
